WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.620
Protocol-aware detection interprets industrial communication beyond addresses and ports.

2
00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:14.280
At a defensive level, engineers need to understand device roles, sessions, reads, writes, commands, errors, and expected communication patterns.

3
00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:21.180
The purpose is not exhaustive protocol decoding; it is enough meaning to recognize security-relevant change.

4
00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:27.700
For Modbus, logic might distinguish routine reads from writes and identify unexpected clients.

5
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:35.560
DNP3 visibility may describe master and outstation relationships, event reporting, or unusual control activity.

6
00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:44.940
OPC UA detections can use application sessions, certificate events, security policy, and expected clients.

7
00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:55.060
EtherNet/IP and PROFINET environments may include cyclic control traffic, explicit configuration, discovery, diagnostics, and engineering functions.

8
00:00:55.160 --> 00:01:03.640
Useful detections focus on role and behavior: a new engineering peer, an unexpected management operation, or communication outside the designed area.

9
00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:07.520
Process meaning cannot be inferred from a function code alone.

10
00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:13.300
A write may be routine during startup, approved during maintenance, and concerning during production.

11
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.640
The same register or tag can represent different equipment across sites.

12
00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:25.260
Asset metadata, process state, work authorization, and engineering knowledge are essential enrichment.

13
00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:29.780
Encrypted or proprietary traffic may limit deep inspection.

14
00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.320
Do not weaken secure communication just to increase visibility.

15
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:41.820
Session metadata, endpoint records, gateway logs, certificate events, and controller change records can provide complementary evidence.

16
00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:49.860
Protocol logic should document parser limitations, expected roles, relevant operations, and confidence.

17
00:01:49.960 --> 00:01:54.440
Avoid brittle assumptions that every uncommon message is malicious.

18
00:01:54.540 --> 00:02:01.500
A strong detection describes a meaningful deviation and gives the analyst enough industrial context to investigate it safely.
